WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.643 --> 00:00:08.060
VALENTIN DELEPLACE:
I am Valentin.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.370
I am a Developer Advocate
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.200
And today we'll talk
about performance.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.630
And we'll talk about performance
of programs written in Go

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.640
running in a public cloud.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.230
I have a lot of slides.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:27.070
And I probably won't have enough
time to answer live questions.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.450
But please come to me just
after the presentation,

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.680
and I'll be happy to discuss.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.690
First, I'll share my insights
about what we want to optimize

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.340
and why.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.770
Then we'll see
how we can observe

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.530
the performance of
a cloud architecture

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.990
and discover which
components best

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:54.400
explains the actual latencies
experienced by our users.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.340
And finally, we'll
have a look at how

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.440
we can observe and improve
our components written in Go.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.140
What is performance tuning?

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.440
Usually it's about working
on something that is slow

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.470
and making it faster,
pretty simple.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.110
Why are we doing
this extra effort?

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.820
Some possible benefits
of improving performance

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.960
is to increase the [INAUDIBLE]
and to decrease the cost.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.690
But today I am focusing on
the most important goal,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.120
which is to offer a
better user experience.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.210
Human seconds are more
precious than machine seconds.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:39.560
Users have very high
expectations of speed,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.230
and if they experience
a sluggish service,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:44.600
they will get frustrated,
and they will leave.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.320
The architecture of a new
software system designed

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.780
for a certain level
of performance

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.850
is always the result of
experience and intuition.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.250
They reflect what
kind of latency

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.980
we should expect in theory.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.940
However, measurements
are more relevant as soon

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.720
as a system exists, even
before its initial release,

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.620
and measurements often
contradict intuition.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.510
I am an experienced
developer, and I

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:20.110
have intuitions where
most of the time

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.930
they are dispelled by
the actual numbers.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.360
So on one hand I get frustrated
that my super-smart intuition

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.830
turned out to be dead wrong.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.430
And on the other hand,
I am happy and grateful

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.750
that my measurements finally
provided me with insights

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.930
closer to the reality.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.810
There is an interesting
correlation between code

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.730
quality and performance.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.310
When you successfully
refactor some code

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.160
to make it faster, you often--

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.400
to make it clearer
and more decoupled,

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.710
you often end up
making it faster

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:00.220
as it's less cluttered with
irrelevant instructions that

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.460
were previously executed
in vain and also

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.510
because some possible
optimization now become more

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.560
obvious and easy to implement.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.990
On the other hand,
if you push further

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.940
in your quest for
performance, you

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.140
will have to give up
simplicity and resort to hacks.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.820
You will indeed shave
some milliseconds--

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.810
this is the green
line going down here--

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.940
but code quality will suffer--

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.820
this is the blue line
also going down now--

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.820
in so far as the
code will become

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.850
more difficult to read
and to reason about, more

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.970
brittle, and less flexible.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:40.960
So it's a tradeoff.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.650
How far are you willing to go?

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.590
Now a word about
automated tests.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:51.340
Designing strong automated
tests is necessary

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.190
in order to make
optimization refactoring

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:59.960
without introducing regression
bugs at the same time.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:02.940
Do you know the difference
between service time

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.730
and response time?

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.350
Anyone?

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:07.370
Not that many people.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:08.420
That's great.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.510
This 100 milliseconds
here is the time

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.200
it took for the service to
process an incoming request.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.610
This is the service time.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.320
But it might very well be
that the latency experienced

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.370
by the user was actually
700 milliseconds.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.940
The user may have
a slow connection

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.640
or the request could be queued
by some data-center front

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.700
end invisible to
the server instance

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.200
until all the other queued
requests have been served.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.750
This total latency
is the response time,

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.240
and it is always bigger
than the service time

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.560
as it also includes the network
round trip and the queuing

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.950
time.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:54.110
In the point of view of your
web server, you may be thinking,

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.800
wow, each of my
resources are served

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.710
in 50 milliseconds or less.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.330
This is really efficient.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:05.050
That's 120 milliseconds
for my total page

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.280
having three resources.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.070
Everything is fine.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:12.260
And in the monitoring system,
all the individual resource

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:15.610
requests from all users
may be displayed together

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.630
in a complex interface.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.810
And when you click
on a single request,

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:23.340
indeed everything looks fine.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.060
Well, not so fast.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.480
What the user really
experiences is certainly not

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.050
a meaningful page load
in 120 milliseconds.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:35.220
Because of the current
design of the page,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.270
the resources are fed by
three sequential requests,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:43.110
and the total response
time is two seconds.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.350
There is room for
improvement, and the best fix

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.590
is probably some
client-server front-end work.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.650
And note the speed improvement
of the existing back end

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.080
endpoints written in Go.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.290
For example, inlining
the most critical assets

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:03.880
could result in a dramatically
better response time.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.910
So why am I saying all
this, and what does it

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.370
have to do with Go programs?

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:12.790
The point is that Go software
is usually server components.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:15.030
The server components
are where we

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.160
have the most
accessible information

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.520
about performance--
the several logs,

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.050
the server traces, et cetera.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.870
But it's too easy
to fool oneself

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:28.290
into thinking that when
the server is fast,

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.810
then magically the
whole system is fast

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:35.250
and the users are having a
pleasant, seamless, responsive

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.330
experience.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.700
That is simply not true.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.000
This is how looking
only at server latencies

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.240
of an internal
application at Google

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.120
I didn't even notice that
an important page was

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.990
taking 30 seconds to render.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:56.080
The users were staring at
the blank screen, completely

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.350
unresponsive while the
JavaScript was performing

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:03.400
UI repaint over and
over for some reason,

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.340
and that was completely
invisible to the server

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.770
monitoring.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.540
The first step to achieve
some meaningful and valuable

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:14.110
performance is a
sane architecture

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.980
that takes into account
the client-device workload,

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:21.130
the browser cache,
the network latency,

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:24.190
and the number of round trips
between server side and client

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.240
side.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.180
Ideally we should
use a warm system

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.300
with user monitoring to
have the browser collect

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.080
valuable metrics so we don't
have to rely only on the server

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.970
metrics.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.210
In the recent years, quite
a lot of the workload

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:43.780
and of the latency has
shifted from the server side

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:49.410
to the client side, and we have
to take this fact into account.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:54.210
More generally, focusing on
the users is a good idea.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:58.680
I can't overemphasize
this axiom, users first.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.920
Now let's have a look at
the observability tooling

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:08.010
available in Google
Cloud Platform.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:11.520
The Cloud web console
includes Stackdriver logging

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.430
which consolidates
the request logs

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.130
and the applicative logs of
your compute components--

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.380
so Compute Engine,
Kubernetes Engine,

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.180
the serverless components--
in near real time.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.700
You can filter the view
by component, by keyword,

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.380
and by date.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.330
So each request, each
line of the request,

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.289
you can see the full URL,
the size of the payload,

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:37.909
the timestamp, and the latency.

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.230
The latency is, of
course, the service time.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.630
It's super useful, but
remember that it's only

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:45.720
a fraction of the response time.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.210
Some of the App Engine
standard requests

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.640
have been automatically
instrumented.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.000
If we click on the blue
link in the latency column,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.450
we will see the waterfall
view of all the API

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:03.050
calls that happened during the
processing of this request.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.360
I love waterfall [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.840
They provide with so much
insight in a single view

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:12.530
that I am even OK when they
are telling me you are wrong.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.530
This is what really happened.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:18.650
The Stackdriver trace
view integrated directly

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.170
into the GCP web
console shows me

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:25.010
for a given request
what API calls were

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.090
made, in which order,
how long they took,

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:33.270
and if they were
sequential or concurrent.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:36.230
The top-right button shows
the applicative logs right

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.790
inside the waterfall diagram.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.870
This is actually a great
feature of observability.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:47.930
The spans tell me what happened,
and the logs tell me why.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:50.540
In this specific example,
the total latency

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.050
of 62 milliseconds
consists almost entirely

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:59.790
in one datastore entity read
and then two datastore queries.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:02.930
We have a few read and
write accesses to memcache,

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.580
but those are super fast.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.450
Now I know that the
bottleneck is the datastore,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.640
and there's no point
in trying to speed up

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.190
my memcache operations.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:16.640
You can also notice that the
last two datastore queries

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:23.080
run concurrently, which is a
good idea when it's possible.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:27.160
I strongly encourage everyone
to set up the automatic export

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.710
of all the logs to BigQuery.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:34.780
Such an export is
also called a sync.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.000
With BigQuery, you don't
have the default retention

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.600
period of 30 days
only, and you can

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.600
use SQL to query
fine-grained performance

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.150
insights over a large
or short period of time.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:49.900
Can we please switch
to the demo computer?

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:56.840
So I will show you
live in my browser.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.810
This is the back end of
a website written in Go

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.180
running on App Engine standard.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.630
In the latency column,
the static assets--

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.440
1 millisecond, 1 millisecond--
have extremely low latencies.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.730
They're actually served
by an internal CDN.

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:26.150
And here if I click on
the blue link of a request

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:31.340
to view the choice details,
I will see the API waterfall.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:35.360
So this one just consists in
two sequential datastore query.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.430
OK, fine, nothing fancy.

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:40.340
If I want to find
something that took longer,

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:45.090
I will filter, show and
trace with higher latency.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.380
Oh, this one was
not really fast.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:49.175
What was it about?

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.350
OK, memcache,
memcache, datastore,

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:57.440
something that can be either
some processing or just

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:02.310
some serverless weirdness.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:05.840
I already have a sync to
BigQuery properly configured.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.220
Here it is.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:13.675
So I can ask questions in
BigQuery like in April for all

00:12:13.675 --> 00:12:16.040
the requests that were
not made by a bot,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:20.060
because I don't care
about search-engine bots,

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.140
and that were not
static assets--

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.910
so they don't start
with this prefix--

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:29.450
what was the request that was
the most frequently accessed

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:34.160
URLs, and what are their average
latency and their highest

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:34.960
latency?

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:39.320
Let's run this SQL
query in BigQuery.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:40.640
We should have an answer.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.140
OK, so the home page slash has
been the most accessed page.

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:48.890
The average latency was
only 5 milliseconds.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.510
No, that's not better.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.510
Only 5 milliseconds,
that's quick.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:57.160
And the max latency was
about 400 milliseconds.

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.260
So secondly most
frequently accessed page

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:05.780
was the RSS endpoint, which
is not really user facing.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:07.520
It's usually fast,
but sometimes it

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.210
can take up to 2
seconds, 1.7 seconds.

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:15.230
Maybe I should have a look to
see if I can speed that up.

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.000
There are some cases
where we'd like

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.990
to insert our own spans
in the trace waterfall--

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:31.010
for example, if we have
a CPU-heavy computation

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:34.630
or to show the duration
between two related events.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:38.720
OpenCensus lets you do this
with its Stackdriver exporter,

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.490
and the Google
Cloud documentation

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:43.460
shows how to do this
for App Engine standard,

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:46.370
for App Engine flex,
for Compute Engine,

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.040
and for Kubernetes Engine.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.630
OpenCensus and its competitor
OpenTracing are open source.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.910
They have recently
joined forces.

00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:59.270
The APIs will
converge, and this is

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.910
great news for the
cloud-computing-observability

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:04.180
community.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.940
Conceptually, the best
view to understand

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:10.090
the latency of a component
is a waterfall view.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:14.140
In this simple example,
100% of the server

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.000
latency of a request--

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.310
service time, no response time--

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:22.270
consists in six sequential
calls to external services.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:24.970
The service works
well, but everyone

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:29.340
agrees that it is a bit
slow, a bit sluggish.

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:33.670
Here, I can see four
opportunities for improvements.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.360
First, I now have a
better idea of the latency

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.890
of each of my dependencies.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.730
If my database is
expected to respond in

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:45.600
under 100 milliseconds
but my measurements show

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.050
that each query actually
take 200 milliseconds,

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.290
then I know that
something should

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.680
be fixed in the
external database,

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.440
maybe create an index.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.620
If I'm lucky, I just sped
up the database accesses

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:04.710
without changing anything
in the server code,

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:08.880
only by doing ops
on the database.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.670
The total latency has
just been reduced a bit.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.530
It's no longer 900 milliseconds.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:17.730
I often witness repeated
access to the same service

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.730
with different
parameters and payload.

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:24.180
It is a law of the universe
that if the service

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.430
accepts batch calls--

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:30.780
that is if it accepts many
commands in a single API call--

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.060
then the batched
call will be faster

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.060
than the sum of the
individual calls.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.510
Here we are calling
service B three times

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:42.150
sequentially with different
parameters one, two, three,

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.870
but we can do better.

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:49.820
By transferring the same amount
of inbound and outbound data

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:54.290
and asking for the same amount
of work to the service B

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.660
but in a single API
call, we suddenly

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.570
improved the total latency.

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:03.770
This may sound counterintuitive,
but it makes perfect sense

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:07.430
because some heavy costs
like the network round

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:10.670
trips, the network latency
component, authentication,

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:15.710
et cetera are now paid only
once instead of three times.

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:18.170
We sometimes achieve
this optimization

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:21.390
by wiping the commands
in a transaction.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.990
However, batching
and transactions

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:25.670
are different concepts.

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:29.330
Transactions are
about atomicity,

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:33.260
and batching is about
achieving economies of scale.

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.630
This is what batching
can look like when

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:42.790
using a Go client library.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:45.590
The code change is
not very complex.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:48.490
We just need to know that it
happens to make things faster.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:55.290
Most of the time it is also true
that making a few expensive API

00:16:55.290 --> 00:17:00.450
calls concurrently is faster
than doing then sequentially.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:01.830
Please note that
I am not talking

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.680
about CPU-bound local
computation here.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:07.619
I am talking about
dispatching concurrent work

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:11.460
to the external world,
by which I mean outside

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:14.069
of my current server component.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.560
In my case, the
calls to service B

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.079
are logically
completely independent

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:22.950
from the database calls and
from the email operation.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:26.190
So I can decide to run service
B concurrently and save

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:29.040
some milliseconds.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.150
This way all the
operations depend

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:33.510
on each other's
completion, so I can't

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:36.680
run them all concurrently.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.710
As the batch call to
service B starts immediately

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:43.670
and is already finished when
the email has been sent,

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.770
it's effectively become free.

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:50.000
The total duration is the
same with or without the call

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.880
to service B.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.220
For this simple
example of paralyzing

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.770
two independent
blocks of computation,

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.780
I suggest to use a WaitGroup.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:03.170
I don't recommend
channels for this

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:07.210
because a channel's details are
subtle and tricky to get right.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:10.020
WaitGroups are straightforward.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:13.190
There are only three
methods to know--

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:17.360
Add to increment,
Done to decrement,

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:20.750
and Wait to let the main
goroutine move on when

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:25.070
all the task have completed.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:29.030
Now please ask yourself if
the email-sending operation

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:34.650
is more synchronous or
asynchronous by nature.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:38.430
In my opinion, if
sending an email

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.780
temporarily fails
for some reason,

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:44.220
then the user can't do
much with that information,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:47.940
and it is the server's job
to retry in a few minutes.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:52.260
I suggest that we add the email
operation to a task queue using

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:57.060
Google Cloud Tasks and then turn
immediately, once again saving

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.670
precious human milliseconds.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:02.190
The task will be
executed asynchronously

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.830
after the HTTP
request has returned,

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:06.987
and it will deal with
with the retry logic

00:19:06.987 --> 00:19:07.820
in case of an error.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.600
Deferring some work
with Cloud Tasks

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.650
has nothing to do with
the Go keyword differ.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:17.080
It's totally different.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:21.480
It's also more code than just
launching a new goroutine.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.600
Creating an asynchronous
task typically

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.480
involves serializing
some parameters

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:30.660
into the payload
of an HTTP request

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:33.730
that will be processed by
another handler of your app.

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:42.430
Now let's pause
and see the result.

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:48.220
A total of 200 milliseconds
instead of 900 is a huge win.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:50.500
You can see that
this was achieved

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:54.250
without using any
low-level assembly code,

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:57.240
without upgrading the
underlying hardware,

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.630
and without even caring if we
have one or many CPU cores.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:08.250
In general, it is most
useful to run the performance

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.890
tests on the same
machine configuration

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:13.140
as used in production.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:17.310
Keep in mind that public clouds
offer virtual machines, which

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:19.920
are convenient
but don't give you

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:23.880
full control over the
underlying hardware.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:27.930
Even if you achieve very
good average latencies,

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.360
you may experience
higher variances

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.640
than with dedicated hardware.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.830
This may perturb the
tests, and in some cases,

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:40.270
this may perturb
the user experience.

