00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:01.040
So with very little
extra configuration,

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:05.590
I can have a profiler agent
collect more information

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:07.900
about what is
going on in the CPU

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:11.410
and in the memory of
the compute instances.

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:15.160
So here I will filter
by today, one day,

00:27:15.160 --> 00:27:18.280
and I will take the top
5% of what happened.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:22.240
Wait, top 5%.

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:29.220
So this is a flame graph of the
mosaic computation, the "Mona

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:31.210
Lisa" with cats that we've seen.

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:33.250
And we'll see in a few
minutes how to read this,

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:36.040
but basically there
is a process function,

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:39.720
and inside the process function
my code is in another function,

00:27:39.720 --> 00:27:40.980
colorizeCat.

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:45.990
And quite a lot of the time
is also spent encoding to PNG.

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:49.510
But that will be
in a few minutes.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:54.270
So cold starts-- they are
a true performance concern

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.610
in serverless applications.

00:27:56.610 --> 00:27:59.100
A cold start is a
request response

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:01.980
that is much higher than
usual because the server is

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:03.460
warming up.

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:07.110
This happens either because
the app has very low traffic

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:10.250
and the first server
instance has to start

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:12.900
or because the
traffic is increasing

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:15.830
and the autoscaler decides
to start an extra instance.

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:23.850
In my experience,
Go applications

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:28.260
tend to have fast cold starts
as they don't need a JVM

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:31.410
and also because, thanks
to the standard library,

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:34.410
they usually don't need to
load hundreds of megabytes

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:36.240
of library dependencies.

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:39.060
For example, my Go apps
deployed an App Engine

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:43.620
standard have cold starts
that are under 2 seconds.

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:49.030
And 2 seconds only, that's
a pretty good cold start.

00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:51.280
The last word about serverless--

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:54.520
we've just announced
Cloud Run this week

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:56.770
which lets you deploy
your own containers

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:01.450
to our serverless platform, and
this is huge on so many levels.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:04.540
More specifically
with Cloud Run on GKE,

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:07.600
you can achieve
vertical scalability

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:11.530
by choosing your custom machines
with more memory and more CPU

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:15.380
calls and still scale to
zero in a serverless way.

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:21.490
Now let's have a look
at the Go tooling.

00:29:21.490 --> 00:29:25.460
The tools that I'm going to show
are not specific to the cloud.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:27.640
They can run on your
development laptop,

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:30.110
and they run on VMs as well.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:33.250
It's perfectly fine to do
some performance exploration

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:35.560
on your own computer,
but remember

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:37.390
that it's more
relevant to run them

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:39.890
in a production environment.

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:41.860
Also, the measurements
in production

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:46.540
sometimes dispel the workstation
measurements as well as

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:49.930
my best intuitions.

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:52.950
In Go, this is how you
run the automated tests.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:55.950
All Go programmers are familiar
with this command line.

