means that in this case,
string.contains is twice

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:59.440
as fast as a regular
expression, which

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:01.270
is good to know if
we want to decide

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.460
which one to put in production.

00:32:04.460 --> 00:32:08.560
Trace-- this is an
execution tracer.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:11.260
It works by instrument
being the program

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:13.450
to produce a binary
file containing

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:17.710
the activity of each CPU
core over a period of time.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:19.660
Is the CPU idle?

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:21.880
Is it running the
garbage collector?

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:24.520
Is it running a function,
and what is the call trace?

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:30.690
This is a CPU-bound
sequential task.

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:34.560
You can see that only one
of the CPU cores is active,

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:36.240
and the other ones are idle.

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:42.420
It turns out that the same
sequential computation

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:44.740
may be dispatched
on several CPU cores

00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:47.760
by the scheduler
of the Go runtime.

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:50.370
This doesn't involve
any concurrency.

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:55.630
Only one CPU core is
working at any given time.

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:59.540
Now this is an example
of concurrent computation

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:01.760
using several goroutines.

00:33:01.760 --> 00:33:04.400
You can see that all
the calls are busy.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:07.850
This can be good or
bad, but it doesn't mean

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:10.070
that the program is efficient.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:13.640
Having a lot of goroutines
communicating and synchronizing

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:17.270
with each other can lead
to contention and to a lot

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:19.832
of expensive context switching.

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:25.610
That wasn't supposed
to happen like that.

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:30.050
Quite often, an inappropriate
granularity for the goroutines

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:33.650
can lead to worse performance
with four CPU cores

00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:37.760
than the same sequential
task on one single CPU core.

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:39.860
The best multicore
performance is

00:33:39.860 --> 00:33:41.990
achieved when each
goroutine performs

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:46.460
a fair amount of sequential
work before writing to a channel

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:50.270
or releasing a mutex or
decrementing a WaitGroup.

00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:54.050
So I'm going to run
them as a benchmark

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:57.320
once again but this
time instrumented them

00:33:57.320 --> 00:34:06.400
with the execution tracer and
outputting a binary file a.out.

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:12.739
By the way, these
are the benchmark

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:17.130
of the mosaic generation
with the cat and "Mona Lisa."

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:17.630
Great.

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:19.030
Now let's view the result--

00:34:19.030 --> 00:34:25.280
go tool trace -http :6060--
that's for my window

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:26.460
on the right--

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:28.040
and the file a.out.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:33.120
So what do we have here?

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:37.840
Quite a lot of information.

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:41.330
So at the top we
can see at any--

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:44.070
so each horizontal
axis is the time.

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:45.190
That's a few seconds.

00:34:45.190 --> 00:34:46.949
That's the duration
of my benchmark

00:34:46.949 --> 00:34:50.710
because my code is executed
over and over for a few seconds.

00:34:50.710 --> 00:34:52.770
So we can see at any
given time how many

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:54.900
goroutines existed here.

00:34:54.900 --> 00:34:59.010
We can see the heaps or how
much memory was actually

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:03.960
consumed in orange and
available to the Go runtime.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:05.280
That's in green.

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:08.670
And we can see how many
operating system threads

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:10.780
are running at this instant.

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:14.820
And below for each
processor, so each CPU core,

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:18.210
we can see if it's busy
or idle, and we can see

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:19.620
what it is doing at that time.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:22.140
So if I click anywhere here--

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:22.920
can we read?

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:24.730
Yes, we can read a little bit.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:28.290
So it was, at that
time, encoding some PNG

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:31.150
using the standard library.

00:35:31.150 --> 00:35:35.410
So if I go to another
slice, you can

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:37.510
see that only one
CPU core is active

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:39.990
because my core is sequential.

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:43.700
So here it's also
encoding some PNG.

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:45.430
Here it's decoding some JPEG.

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:48.140
Oh yes, the input "Mona
Lisa," that was JPEG.

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:54.210
And there are four
lines of processor

00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:56.860
because I have four
CPU cores on my laptop.

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:06.290
Now Pprof.

00:36:06.290 --> 00:36:09.780
Pprof is the profiling
data format available out

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:11.680
of the box in Go.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:14.175
Let's have a look at
the Pprof CPU profiler.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:21.480
The CPU profiling
works by sampling.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:24.780
It includes a relatively
small runtime overhead.

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:28.380
Every now and then the kernel
sends a SIGPROF signal,

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:30.780
and the special handler
of the Go runtime

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:35.500
gather the call trace of one
currently running goroutine.

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:39.390
The data set of all these call
traces over a period of time

00:36:39.390 --> 00:36:42.810
contains rich
statistical information

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:46.410
that is used to produce a
graph of the function showing

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:51.780
who calls who and how much
time is spent in each function.

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:54.930
In the resulting graph,
each box is a function

00:36:54.930 --> 00:36:57.330
and explicitly
displays the number

00:36:57.330 --> 00:37:01.800
of seconds spent inside the code
of the function, the total time

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:05.830
spent in the function, including
its own function calls,

00:37:05.830 --> 00:37:09.060
and the equivalent percentage
of the total execution time

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:10.490
recorded.

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:13.590
Running the benchmarks
is a very effective way

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:16.650
to gather this information
as the functions are

00:37:16.650 --> 00:37:19.440
called many times over
a short period of time.

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:25.050
At the top, you will find
the program's entry point

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:28.320
and your own
important functions.

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:31.590
And at the bottom are all the
standard library functions

00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:34.620
and the system calls.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.260
The duration of
each box consists

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:42.300
in the sum of its own
CPU time plus all the CPU

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:45.060
times of the function it calls.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:48.330
This counts only the time
actually spent using the CPU,

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:51.175
not the time spent
waiting for something.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:38:01.290
So once again I will
run the benchmarks,

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:07.770
this time instrumented with
the CPU profiler of Pprof

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:10.080
to a binary file a.prof.

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:14.140
And it's important to not
care at all about the results,

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:15.900
the number from the benchmarks.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:18.810
These numbers are skewed
by instrumentation.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:24.100
Here, I am only interested
into the visualization.

00:38:24.100 --> 00:38:26.620
go tool pprof, OK.

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:31.000
-http :6060, that's for
my window on the right,

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:32.400
and the binary file a.prof.

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:39.290
So what can we see?

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:41.600
We can see that,
in my benchmark,

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:44.930
the process function,
which creates the mosaic

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:48.350
took 90% of the total time.

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:54.320
My own code, colorizeCat,
it took only 19%

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:55.790
of the total time.

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:59.780
And the PNG encoding, which is
a part of the standard library

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:05.240
and which happens only at
the end of the processing,

00:39:05.240 --> 00:39:10.880
it accounts for 53%
of the total time.

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:13.940
This is highly valuable insight.

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:16.550
If I wanted to
optimize my own code,

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:21.830
I would only be able to optimize
less than 20% of the workload.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:25.040
The bottleneck is the
standard library function

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:28.040
of PNG encoding,
which is not my code

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:31.490
and which I can't really
parallelize on multiple cores

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:33.860
because it is called
only once at the end

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:35.210
of the mosaic generation.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:44.440
However, now that I know that
the encoding is the bottleneck,

00:39:44.440 --> 00:39:47.490
I decide to not
buffer the output.

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:51.060
The only advantage of
buffering in my previous code

00:39:51.060 --> 00:39:54.000
was to log the precise
number of bytes written.

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:56.700
That was the length
of the byte buffer.

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:02.370
If I give the response
writer directly

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:04.780
to the process
function, the browser

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:08.730
now receives the streamed
data as it is being encoded.

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:12.180
This is a good UX feedback
because the user sees

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:16.050
the first kitties displayed
much sooner than before,

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:19.590
and it also greatly improves
the total response time

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:22.200
because the encoding
is effectively

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:25.170
parallelized with the
response data being

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:26.760
downloaded by the client.

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:32.830
I'm missing a slide.

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:35.980
OK, no problem.

00:40:35.980 --> 00:40:39.250
So as I mentioned
earlier, everyone

00:40:39.250 --> 00:40:42.640
has intuitions about
expected performance.

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:45.820
There is also sometimes
some emotional investments

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:48.250
and some prejudice
on this topic.

00:40:48.250 --> 00:40:51.430
I suggest to stick to
the facts and to discover

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:53.080
the current bottleneck.

00:40:53.080 --> 00:40:56.240
What takes the most
time in my use case?

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:59.170
Everything that is not
the bottleneck can wait.

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:03.340
Now the flame graphs.

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:07.310
A flame graph is a fancy way to
visualize the profiling data.

00:41:07.310 --> 00:41:13.140
It was invented by Brenden
Gregg and around 2013.

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:19.710
So I will run just the
same command as before,

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:25.590
go tool pprof with -http :6060.

00:41:25.590 --> 00:41:27.630
I cannot just rerun the same.

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:30.540
I have to write it
all over, and a.prof.

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:38.160
So if I click in
View, Flame Graph,

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:40.100
I have this, which
is really nice.

00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:42.760
It is not completely
of use the first time

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:45.440
we see a flame graph,
but the horizontal

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:49.630
axis x doesn't represent
the chronological time.

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:52.420
However, the widths
of each span is

00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:54.970
proportional to
the total duration

00:41:54.970 --> 00:41:57.680
spent executing this function.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:00.580
So here I have two
benchmarks, benchmark process

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:02.800
and benchmark process in memory.

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:04.960
If I click on this
one, I can see

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:09.880
that my own code colorizeCat
is only part of the total.

00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:13.390
Decoding the JPEG of
"Mona Lisa" is like 15%,

00:42:13.390 --> 00:42:17.600
and the PNG encoding at the end
accounts for more than half.

00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:21.400
So if I am curious and I want to
know what the standard library

00:42:21.400 --> 00:42:23.560
is doing while
writing the image,

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:25.870
I can dig deeper and
deeper because it

00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:29.200
is really great to find the
root cause of some latency.

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:37.520
Do we have at least
seven minutes?

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:38.170
Yes we do.

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:38.670
OK.

00:42:45.270 --> 00:42:47.670
Why should I look
at a flame graph

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:50.160
if it basically displays
the same information

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:53.340
as the regular Pprof graph?

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:56.050
This is a
stereotypical workday--

00:42:56.050 --> 00:43:01.260
work in the morning, work in the
afternoon, and sleep all night.

00:43:01.260 --> 00:43:04.260
And this is a
stereotypical vacation day.

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:07.200
Sleep in the morning, go to
the beach in the afternoon,

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:10.720
and dance all night.

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:13.000
And this is what the
Pprof graph looks

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:15.370
like if you run a
simulation containing

00:43:15.370 --> 00:43:19.090
some people in activity and
some people in vacation.

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:21.700
Some functions--
for example, sleep--

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:27.150
are called by everyone but not
at the same time of the day.

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:30.650
Even with a complete set
of duration annotations,

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:33.120
this graph doesn't
give enough information

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:36.450
to know if going to the
beach, the yellow box,

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:42.120
is part of work day or a part
of vacation days or both.

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:46.720
Maybe some employees
prefer to skip work.

00:43:46.720 --> 00:43:51.100
This flame graph built out of
the same profiling information

00:43:51.100 --> 00:43:52.990
quickly gives the answer.

00:43:52.990 --> 00:43:57.850
The beach activity on the
right is properly stacked only

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:01.100
under the vacation-day box.

00:44:01.100 --> 00:44:05.320
And we also discover that a
vacation day typically involves

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:08.920
a bit more sleep than a workday.

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:11.110
So the bottom line
is flame graphs

00:44:11.110 --> 00:44:13.810
are very beautiful
like a campfire

00:44:13.810 --> 00:44:16.240
and also super useful
to find the root

00:44:16.240 --> 00:44:20.350
cause of latency issues.

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:22.570
If you suspect that the
performance of your program

00:44:22.570 --> 00:44:25.870
is not correctly explained
by trace and Pprof,

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:28.450
you may want to use
the perf tool, which

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:32.220
is not specific to Go.

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:35.940
Now concurrency--
concurrency and parallelism

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:39.300
are powerful tools to do
several things at once

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:42.990
and help decrease the total
latency of your request.

00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:46.260
As we've seen, a lot of the
time spent in a web service

00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:49.380
is spent waiting for
an API call to return.

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:52.470
In this case, the performance
is not related at all

00:44:52.470 --> 00:44:54.510
to the number of
CPU cores and not

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:57.390
related to the speed
of each CPU core.

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:59.400
It is, however,
extremely useful to be

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:05.560
able to call several
external APIs concurrently.

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:07.490
I have to be quick on this one.

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:09.520
This command, it runs a test.

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:13.230
Who has ever run the
data race detector?

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:15.010
OK, you already know about it.

00:45:15.010 --> 00:45:16.780
It's super useful
to stay on track

00:45:16.780 --> 00:45:19.810
because when you work on
performance optimization,

00:45:19.810 --> 00:45:22.300
sometimes you introduce
concurrencies and channels

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:25.150
and goroutines, and you
introduce subtle bugs,

00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:27.640
and it is really useful
to detect those bugs

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:29.090
as soon as possible.

00:45:29.090 --> 00:45:32.200
There is no such thing as
a harmless data race in Go.

00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:35.680
We have to be careful.

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:39.520
Here is a quick checklist of
common performance bottlenecks

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:42.370
and how we can
advise them in Go.

00:45:42.370 --> 00:45:46.780
The ever-popular execution
of useless instructions--

00:45:46.780 --> 00:45:49.600
obviously hunting and
eliminating useless code

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:51.250
is a good idea.

00:45:51.250 --> 00:45:54.150
Please do it.

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:57.440
Now network, IU, and
external API calls

00:45:57.440 --> 00:46:01.250
are relatively
extremely expensive.

00:46:01.250 --> 00:46:05.480
My advice is to make as
few requests as possible

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:08.610
and not to block everything
while waiting for a network

00:46:08.610 --> 00:46:09.110
response.

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:14.570
Reading from and writing
to the file system

00:46:14.570 --> 00:46:21.210
is slower than most of what we
do in memory and in the CPU.

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:25.490
So my advice is to work as
much as you can in memory

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:29.890
and to do your I/O
in a nonblocking way.

00:46:29.890 --> 00:46:34.140
I know some are taking pictures,
but I am running out of time.

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:37.380
Contention-- thousands
of goroutines

00:46:37.380 --> 00:46:40.350
waiting for each other
is a perfect recipe

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:42.420
for unnecessary sluggishness.

00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:49.990
To limit the excessive need
for constant communication

00:46:49.990 --> 00:46:53.260
between goroutines, I
suggest to create workers.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:55.120
The worker pattern,
it look like this.

00:46:55.120 --> 00:46:58.630
If I have four CPU cores
and 1 million computations

00:46:58.630 --> 00:47:01.750
to perform, yes, I could
launch 1 million goroutines,

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.590
but that would be a little
bit heavy and costly.

00:47:04.590 --> 00:47:08.460
It's more efficient to start
a small number of worker

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:14.530
goroutinees, say eight, and have
each worker process only 1/8

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:18.300
of the total load sequentially.

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.730
Asking for memory to
the operating system

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:23.610
is a notoriously slow operation.

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:27.600
My advice is to allocate
large chunks of memory

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:30.930
in a single call to the
built-in make function,

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:34.620
to use memory by passing
both buffers as arguments

00:47:34.620 --> 00:47:39.540
to the processing functions, and
also using a pool from the sync

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:42.010
package can prove very useful.

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:48.830
Heap or stack-- this part is
specific to the Go compiler.

00:47:48.830 --> 00:47:51.950
We know that allocating
in the heap is expensive

00:47:51.950 --> 00:47:54.930
and that allocating on
the stack is almost free,

00:47:54.930 --> 00:47:57.850
but what goes in the heap
and what goes to the stack?

00:47:57.850 --> 00:48:00.590
The answer is not intuitive.

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:03.020
Some local variable
may escape to the heap

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:05.930
if we take its reference
and pass it along.

00:48:05.930 --> 00:48:09.080
In the end, it depends if
the compiler is clever enough

00:48:09.080 --> 00:48:11.930
to prove that a variable
doesn't escape so it

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:14.690
is safe to put it on the stack.

00:48:14.690 --> 00:48:16.750
These commands
prove scientifically

00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:18.500
that the core
specification does not

00:48:18.500 --> 00:48:21.610
contain the word stack or heap.

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:27.170
Use this extra gcflag,
-m, to determine which

00:48:27.170 --> 00:48:29.810
local variables escape or not.

00:48:29.810 --> 00:48:32.060
You may then try to
adapt the function

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:36.230
scope to leak less references.

00:48:36.230 --> 00:48:39.050
Regular expressions are
great, but they are often

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:41.510
overkill for a given need.

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:46.230
So my advice is basically
to avoid them when you can.

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:49.470
Just two seconds
on the next slide.

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:53.730
Function calls-- a function
call incur nonzero cost, which

00:48:53.730 --> 00:48:57.240
can be significant if the
function is very small

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:59.700
and they're called
millions of times.

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:03.620
So the specification does not
talk at all about inlining,

00:49:03.620 --> 00:49:07.830
and the compiler has its own
very specific internal rules

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:11.130
when it comes to decide if a
small function will be inline

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:12.240
or not.

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:16.800
Chasing the inliner to have it
inline a specific hot function

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:19.260
has become a popular sport.

00:49:19.260 --> 00:49:21.390
Also, these
implementation details

00:49:21.390 --> 00:49:26.130
are subject to change from
a Go release to another.

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:28.650
Before I finish, I'd like
to thank my colleague, David

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:31.110
Gageot, for his wonderful
presentation tool

00:49:31.110 --> 00:49:34.110
DemoIt, which I am using today.

00:49:34.110 --> 00:49:37.270
And also why do I have
two remote clickers?

00:49:37.270 --> 00:49:39.950
Because I'm worth it.

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:41.510
Here is my takeaway.

00:49:41.510 --> 00:49:43.880
The Go language was
designed for cloud computing

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:45.710
with scalability in mind.

00:49:45.710 --> 00:49:49.130
Its ecosystem is rich, and
the performance tooling

00:49:49.130 --> 00:49:50.750
is really good.

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:55.220
Now to improve the experience
of your users and make the world

00:49:55.220 --> 00:49:56.600
a better place.

00:49:56.600 --> 00:49:59.420
Start with an
important use case.

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:01.520
Discover the bottleneck.

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:04.310
Fix it, and repeat.

00:50:04.310 --> 00:50:05.910
Thank you very
much for listening.

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:07.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:07.110 --> 00:50:11.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

